  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 128 AM CDT Wed Sep 2 2026\n\nAs of 1 am CDT, there are some lingering showers over portions of \nnortheastern SD and west central MN. These showers will be \ndissipating and moving out into the early morning. Other than the \nlingering showers this morning, there could be some localized areas \nof patchy fog. The easterly flow this morning could create upslope \nfog where the flow tries to move up over the terrain in areas of \nhigher humidity. This fog will dissipate during the morning once the \nsun rises and the atmosphere starts to mix things up. \n\nThe moisture will be sticking around, as relative humidity values \nwill stay above 55 percent for most of central and northeastern SD \nand west central MN today. This moisture as well as some higher \ninstability, between 1000-2000 J/kg possible late this afternoon \ninto the evening, will help to create a decent environment for storm \nand shower development. The one thing that will be lacking is a \nsource of lift. Some models show a shortwave moving over SD late \nthis evening and overnight, which would help with the development as \na source of lift. However, there is variability in the models with \nthe shortwave, as some of them don't have it moving over until \nThursday evening and other models don't have any shortwave move \nover. Without the shortwave as a source of lift, the showers and \nstorms will struggle to develop. There is also variability in the \nmodels as to how long the storms will be able to last if they do \nform. This is because some of the models hint at a low level jet \ndeveloping overnight to support the showers and storms, while other \nmodels don't have the jet develop and the storms don't last. If \nthings line up just right, there could be some isolated showers and \nstorms late this evening and overnight into early Thursday morning. \nModels show lapse rates between 6-7 C/km and bulk shear up around 35 \nkts, which could help with some hail development and occasional \nstrong wind gust tonight if the storms are able to happen, though \nthere is lower confidence in this happening. \n\nSoutherly flow Thursday will help to warm the temperatures to be in \nthe mid 80s into the low 90s, which is 10-15 degrees warmer than \nnormal. These southerly winds will also help to push moist air into \ncentral and northeastern SD and west central MN. A frontal boundary \nis forecast to move through SD Thursday and overnight, which will \nhelp to provide the lift needed for storms and showers to develop in \nthe moist environment. The models show the instability developing \nthrough the day mainly over eastern SD and west central MN, which is \nin front of the frontal boundary, with CAPE values of 1000-3000 \nJ/kg. Depending on how fast or slow the boundary moves into and \nthrough SD during the day, there could be a smaller or larger area \nwith chances for showers and storms to develop. The NAM shows some \nhigher mid-level lapse rates between 6-7 C/km and bulk shear of 30-\n45 kts, which could help with the development of some hail and \nstronger winds if anything were to develop. There does not look to \nbe the development of a low level jet Thursday night, which might \nalso limit the ability for the storms to stay alive through the \nnight. There is a Slight (level 2 of 5) to a Marginal (level 1 of 5) \nrisk for these storms to become severe. The primary threats \ninitially look to be hail up to 2 inches in diameter and wind gusts \nof 60 to 75 mph. As the evening progresses, the storms will likely \ntransition to more of a wind threat as they could become more \nlinear. \n\nAfter Thursday, an upper-level ridge looks to build over and \nslightly to the east of SD. This will help warm air to build in \nFriday and Saturday, causing temperatures to be 10-20 degrees warmer \nthan normal. High temperatures Friday and Saturday are forecast to \nbe in the upper 80s to mid 90s, warmest over central SD. The ridge \nshould also help to keep showers and storm from developing, though \nsome models have a shortwave pushing through Friday night into \nSaturday morning, which could support an isolated shower/storm or \ntwo to developing.",
